## 4. Admin-Customer Correspondence Rule

When implementing any feature that touches the admin panel, ALWAYS reason through BOTH sides:

```
Scenario: [Feature Name]
Admin Side: [What the admin creates / manages]
Customer Side: [What the customer experiences]
Data Flow: [How data moves between admin → API → customer]
Edge Cases: [Potential issues]
```

**Established Admin-Customer Pairs:**
- `MenuItemForm` ↔ `MenuItem` — Admin creates menu items; customers browse and order
- `PlatterForm` ↔ `PlatterItem` — Admin configures platters; customers customize
- `OrdersList` ↔ `OrderTracking` — Admin manages and updates orders; customers track live status
- `TableManagement` ↔ `TableSelection` — Admin assigns tables; customers select on arrival

---

## 5. Order Lifecycle & Real-Time Rules

```
Customer Places Order
       ↓
POST /api/orders → Save to MongoDB (status: "pending")
       ↓
Socket.IO emits "new_order" → Admin Dashboard updates live
       ↓
Admin accepts → PATCH /api/updateorderstatus
       ↓
Socket.IO emits "order_updated" → Customer progress bar updates
       ↓
Twilio WhatsApp confirmation sent to customer
```

- **Never** block order creation on Twilio failures — wrap all Twilio calls in `try/catch`.
- **Always** emit Socket.IO events AFTER successful DB writes, not before.
- **Always** validate order payload with a Zod schema before touching MongoDB.

---

## 6. Variation System Standards (CLK)

The variation engine is CLK's core differentiator. Treat it with care:

```typescript
// Mongoose Schema Shape
interface VariationGroup {
  title: string;
  isRequired: boolean;
  options: Array<{ name: string; additionalPrice: number }>;
}

interface MenuItem {
  name: string;
  basePrice: number;
  variations: VariationGroup[];
}
```

- Never flatten variation options — always preserve the nested `VariationGroup[]` structure.
- `additionalPrice` is always additive on top of `basePrice`.
- Validate that required variation groups have at least one option selected before allowing add-to-cart.

---

## 7. Code Style & Quality Rules

- **TypeScript strict mode** — no `any` types unless absolutely unavoidable (and documented why).
- **Component structure**: Functional components only; use custom hooks (`useVariationSelector`, `useCart`) for complex logic.
- **Naming conventions**:
  - Components: `PascalCase` (e.g., `MenuItemCard.tsx`)
  - Hooks: `camelCase` with `use` prefix (e.g., `useOrderTracking.ts`)
  - API routes (Pages Router): `kebab-case` (e.g., `update-order-status.ts`)
  - Mongoose models: `PascalCase` (e.g., `Order.ts`)
- **No `console.log`** in production code. Use proper error boundaries and structured error returns.
- **ESLint must pass** before any commit.

---

## 8. How to Document Progress

Whenever you write or modify code:
1. Log the update details in [`documentation/status.md`](file:///d:/ordering-system/documentation/status.md) and [`documentation/memory.md`](file:///d:/ordering-system/documentation/memory.md).
2. Ensure architectural decisions comply with [`documentation/architecture.md`](file:///d:/ordering-system/documentation/architecture.md).
3. Record changes adhering to guidelines in [`documentation/documentation.md`](file:///d:/ordering-system/documentation/documentation.md).
4. For project-specific feature details, update the relevant docs inside the sub-project's own `docs/` folder.
